{"name":"Mountain Stream","hex":"#96afb7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mountain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elevation of land of considerable dimensions rising more or less abruptly, forming a conspicuous figure in the landscape, usually having a small extent of surface at its summit. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Something very large in size or quantity; a huge amount; a great heap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountain"},{"term":"stream","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small river; a large creek; a body of moving water confined by banks.","All moving waters."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To flow in a continuous or steady manner, like a liquid.","To extend; to stretch out with a wavy motion; to float in the wind."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stream"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mountain stream","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain stream is a brook or stream, usually with a steep gradient, flowing down a mountainside. Its swift flow rate often transports large quantities of rock, gravel, soil, wood or even entire logs with the stream. The main characteristic of mountain streams in the Alpine region is their steep gradient and sharply varying rates of flow within a short period of time, as a result of snowmelt and sudden storms. Streams of such nature are rarer in gently rolling countryside, although they may occur on scarp slopes where, although the height difference is not so great, they tend to have a larger catchment area than mountain streams."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Weissbach_geroellsperren.jpg/330px-Weissbach_geroellsperren.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_stream","wikidata":"Q2570370"},{"title":"Mountain stream salamander","blocks":[{"text":"The mountain stream salamander or mountain stream siredon is a species of mole salamander that only lives in central Mexico."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Ambystoma_altamirani_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_stream_salamander","wikidata":"Q2512350"},{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"},{"title":"Stream","blocks":[{"text":"A stream is a continuous body of surface water flowing within the bed and banks of a channel. The channel is a watercourse, although the watercourse may be seasonally or periodically dry. Depending on its location or certain characteristics, a stream may be referred to by a variety of local or regional names. A long, large stream is usually called a river, while a smaller, less voluminous, and more intermittent stream is known as a creek or tributary, among other names."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg/330px-%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stream","wikidata":"Q47521"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Waldbach-WJP.jpg/500px-Waldbach-WJP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Waldbach-WJP.jpg/500px-Waldbach-WJP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Muglbach in the Upper Palatine Forest of Bavaria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWaldbach-WJP.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chimborazo, Ecuador, whose summit is the point farthest away from the Earth's center[5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVolc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png/500px-Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png/1280px-Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p